Experiment 3: Bubble Sort with Big-O Analysis
Aim

To sort an array using Bubble Sort and analyse its time complexity.

Algorithm
Compare each pair of adjacent elements in the array.
If the left element is greater than the right one, swap them.
After each pass, the largest remaining element moves to its correct position at the end.
Repeat for n-1 passes.
If a pass makes no swaps, the array is already sorted, so stop early.
Pseudo code
BubbleSort(A, n)
  for i = 0 to n-2
      swapped = 0
      for j = 0 to n-i-2
          if A[j] > A[j+1]
              swap A[j], A[j+1]
              swapped = 1
      if swapped == 0
          break
Source code (C)
c
#include <stdio.h>

int main() {
    int a[] = {64, 34, 25, 12, 22, 11, 90};
    int n = 7, i, j, temp, swapped;
    int comparisons = 0, swaps = 0, passes = 0;

    printf("Original array: ");
    for (i = 0; i < n; i++)
        printf("%d ", a[i]);
    printf("\n");

    for (i = 0; i < n - 1; i++) {
        swapped = 0;
        passes++;
        for (j = 0; j < n - i - 1; j++) {
            comparisons++;
            if (a[j] > a[j + 1]) {
                temp = a[j];
                a[j] = a[j + 1];
                a[j + 1] = temp;
                swaps++;
                swapped = 1;
            }
        }
        if (swapped == 0)
            break;
    }

    printf("Sorted array  : ");
    for (i = 0; i < n; i++)
        printf("%d ", a[i]);
    printf("\n");

    printf("Passes = %d, Comparisons = %d, Swaps = %d\n", passes, comparisons, swaps);

    return 0;
}
Output
Original array: 64 34 25 12 22 11 90 
Sorted array  : 11 12 22 25 34 64 90 
Passes = 6, Comparisons = 21, Swaps = 14
Result

The array was sorted successfully using Bubble Sort.

Time complexity: Worst and average case O(n²); best case O(n) (already sorted array, thanks to the swapped flag).
Space complexity: O(1), since sorting is done in place.
Bubble Sort is stable.
